                <h2>The Suspension Notice at 2 AM</h2>
<p>At 2:17 AM, Lao Zhang, the performance marketing lead at a tool app company, received a Google Ads system email: the account was permanently suspended due to "invalid traffic policy violations."</p>
<p>The account still had approximately $12,000 in balance. Google Play Store was running user acquisition campaigns, delivering around 800 daily converted users. The team had a product launch event scheduled for 10 AM the same day, with all creative materials already lined up as planned.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. According to Google Ads Policy team data, account suspensions due to invalid traffic accounted for 34% of all suspensions in 2023. A significant portion stems from natural traffic misattribution within the attribution window, device changes causing duplicate clicks, and discrepancies between SDK analytics and Google Ads measurement.</p>
<h2>Problem Analysis: Why Was the Account Suspended</h2>
<h3>Surface Reasons vs. Underlying Logic</h3>
<p>The official Google Ads email indicated the violation type as "invalid traffic—likely to increase advertiser spend." However, Lao Zhang's team showed all traffic metrics were normal in Google Analytics backend, with conversion rates holding steady at 12%-15% and user retention data aligning with healthy benchmarks for tool apps.</p>
<p><strong>The root cause lies in information asymmetry</strong>: Google Ads' risk control system is based on platform-wide data, while advertisers can only see data within the attribution window. When attribution discrepancies exist between third-party analytics tools and Google Ads, the system triggers suspicious traffic flags.</p>
<h3>Three High-Frequency Suspension Reasons</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Suspension Type</th>
<th>Trigger Scenario</th>
<th>Detection Difficulty</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Invalid Traffic</td>
<td>Natural traffic misattribution, device ID changes, SDK data discrepancies</td>
<td>★★★</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Circumventing Systems</td>
<td>Landing page redirects, forced downloads, malware</td>
<td>★★</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Content Policy</td>
<td>Exaggerated claims, sensitive terms, copyrighted materials</td>
<td>★</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>Preparing Appeal Materials: 48-Hour Sprint</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Evidence Chain Compilation (0-8 hours)</h3>
<p>Lao Zhang's team first exported the following data:</p>
<ul>
<li>Last 30 days of Google Ads campaign reports (hourly granularity)</li>
<li>Google Analytics conversion path analysis</li>
<li>Retention data from third-party attribution platform (Adjust)</li>
<li>User device distribution report</li>
<li>App Store/Google Play backend data</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Core logic</strong>: The only way to prove traffic authenticity is cross-validation with multi-source data. When a single data source shows anomalies, Google Ads tends to trust its own algorithm. But when multiple independent platforms show consistent trends, the likelihood of misjudgment decreases significantly.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Writing the Appeal Letter (8-24 hours)</h3>
<p>The appeal letter structure determines the reviewer's reading experience and judgment efficiency:</p>
<pre><code>【Paragraph 1: Clear Request】
Account ID: xxx-xxx-xxxx
Request Type: Account Reinstatement
Violation Reason: Invalid Traffic Policy

【Paragraph 2: Situation Explanation】
We noticed recent conversion attribution data differs from Google Ads, primarily because...

【Paragraph 3: Evidence Attachments】
Attachment 1: GA conversion path analysis (proving users genuinely completed conversions)
Attachment 2: Adjust retention data (third-party independent verification)
Attachment 3: Technical documentation (explaining SDK attribution discrepancy reasons)

【Paragraph 4: Remediation Commitment】
We will take the following measures to prevent similar issues...
</code></pre>
<h3>Step 3: Submission and Follow-up (24-48 hours)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Submit through official appeal portal, retain submission record screenshots</li>
<li>Post synchronously on Google Ads Help Community (increase visibility)</li>
<li>Send follow-up email after 48 hours, noting the Case ID</li>
</ul>
<h2>Results and Lessons Learned</h2>
<p>42 hours after submitting the appeal, Lao Zhang received the reinstatement notice. The account balance was preserved, and campaigns resumed normal operation.</p>
<p><strong>Key factors for this successful appeal</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Multi-source data support</strong>: GA + Adjust + Google Play data formed a complete evidence chain</li>
<li><strong>Comprehensive technical explanation</strong>: Clearly explained the underlying reasons for attribution discrepancies</li>
<li><strong>Fast response</strong>: Completed material preparation within 24 hours of suspension, reducing reviewer concerns about "fund-grabbing flight"</li>
</ol>
<h2>Three Recommendations to Reduce Suspension Risk</h2>
<ul>
<li>Regularly compare conversion data between Google Ads and third-party platforms; proactively investigate when discrepancies exceed 15%</li>
<li>Add independent verification scripts for Google Ads conversion tracking code on landing pages</li>
<li>Account flags often have warning signs before suspension: CTR abnormal decline, invalid traffic percentage sudden increase—communicate proactively with support team</li>
</ul>
<p>Account appeals aren't "luck-based"—they're an efficiency battle of evidence preparation. Having complete materials ready is more important than submitting frequently.</p>
